  ('Classical composers and their works', 'adult', 'mc', 'Which composer wrote the celebrated set of keyboard variations known as the ''Goldberg Variations'', published in 1741?', '{"options":["George Frideric Handel","Johann Sebastian Bach","Domenico Scarlatti","Georg Philipp Telemann"],"correctIndex":1}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"The Goldberg Variations (BWV 988) were composed by J.S. Bach, published in 1741 as part of the Clavier-Übung IV; all three distractors are contemporaries of Bach but had no connection to this work."}'::jsonb),
  ('Classical composers and their works', 'adult', 'mc', 'Ludwig van Beethoven dedicated his ''Eroica'' Symphony — originally intended for Napoleon Bonaparte — to which Austrian nobleman after withdrawing that dedication?', '{"options":["Prince Lobkowitz","Prince Metternich","Archduke Rudolf","Count Rasumovsky"],"correctIndex":0}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"Beethoven''s Symphony No. 3 \"Eroica\" (Op. 55) was ultimately dedicated to Prince Joseph Franz Maximilian Lobkowitz, a Bohemian/Austrian nobleman and patron, after Beethoven famously scratched out the dedication to Napoleon; the other options are real figures associated with Beethoven but not the dedicatee of the Eroica."}'::jsonb),
  ('Classical composers and their works', 'adult', 'mc', 'Hector Berlioz''s ''Symphonie fantastique'' of 1830 is structured around a recurring melodic idea he called the ''idée fixe''. What does that term literally mean in French?', '{"options":["Hidden theme","Fixed idea","Recurring dream","Obsessive love"],"correctIndex":1}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"\"Idée fixe\" literally translates from French as \"fixed idea,\" and all other options (hidden theme, recurring dream, obsessive love) are clearly incorrect as literal translations."}'::jsonb),
  ('Classical composers and their works', 'adult', 'mc', 'Which of Mozart''s operas is set in a single day inside the palace of Count Almaviva and is based on a play by Pierre Beaumarchais?', '{"options":["Così fan tutte","Don Giovanni","The Magic Flute","The Marriage of Figaro"],"correctIndex":3}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"The Marriage of Figaro (Le nozze di Figaro, 1786) is indeed set over a single day in Count Almaviva''s palace and is based on Beaumarchais''s play La Folle Journée, ou le Mariage de Figaro; the other three options are clearly wrong."}'::jsonb),
  ('Classical composers and their works', 'adult', 'mc', 'Frédéric Chopin wrote almost exclusively for which solo instrument throughout his compositional career?', '{"options":["Violin","Harpsichord","Piano","Cello"],"correctIndex":2}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"Chopin is famously known for composing almost exclusively for the piano; the other options (violin, harpsichord, cello) are clearly wrong as Chopin wrote virtually nothing for them as solo instruments."}'::jsonb),
  ('Classical composers and their works', 'adult', 'mc', 'Dmitri Shostakovich''s String Quartet No. 8 in C minor, written in 1960, is saturated with a four-note musical monogram. Whose initials does that monogram represent?', '{"options":["Joseph Stalin","Shostakovich himself","Lenin","His wife Nina Varzar"],"correctIndex":1}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"The four-note motif D–E♭–C–B (D. SCH in German musical notation) stands for Dmitri SCHostakovich, his own initials, and is woven throughout the quartet."}'::jsonb),
  ('20th-century world history', 'adult', 'mc', 'The 1919 Treaty of Versailles held Germany solely responsible for World War I under which notorious clause?', '{"options":["Article 48","Article 231","Article 10","Article 14"],"correctIndex":1}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles is indeed the infamous \"War Guilt Clause\" that held Germany solely responsible for WWI; the other options are clearly wrong (Article 48 was the emergency powers clause of the Weimar Constitution, not the Treaty)."}'::jsonb),
  ('20th-century world history', 'adult', 'mc', 'Which Soviet leader initiated the policy of ''glasnost'' — meaning openness — as part of his broader reform programme in the 1980s?', '{"options":["Yuri Andropov","Leonid Brezhnev","Mikhail Gorbachev","Konstantin Chernenko"],"correctIndex":2}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"Mikhail Gorbachev introduced glasnost (openness) alongside perestroika (restructuring) as part of his reform programme after becoming General Secretary in 1985; the other three leaders are all clearly wrong."}'::jsonb),
  ('20th-century world history', 'adult', 'mc', 'The 1947 partition of British India into two independent nations was carried out under the framework of which plan?', '{"options":["The Cripps Mission","The Wavell Plan","The Mountbatten Plan","The Cabinet Mission Plan"],"correctIndex":2}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"The 1947 partition of British India was carried out under the Mountbatten Plan (announced on June 3, 1947), which led to the Indian Independence Act 1947; the other options — Cripps Mission (1942), Wavell Plan (1945), and Cabinet Mission Plan (1946) — were earlier, distinct proposals that did not result in partition."}'::jsonb),
  ('20th-century world history', 'adult', 'mc', 'During the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, a Soviet submarine nearly launched a nuclear torpedo after mistakenly believing war had begun — which officer''s refusal to authorise the launch is widely credited with averting catastrophe?', '{"options":["Vasili Arkhipov","Anatoly Dobrynin","Valentin Savitsky","Georgy Malenkov"],"correctIndex":0}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"Vasili Arkhipov was the flotilla commander aboard Soviet submarine B-59 who refused to authorize the launch of a nuclear torpedo on October 27, 1962, when captain Valentin Savitsky wanted to fire; all three distractors are clearly wrong — Dobrynin was the Soviet ambassador to the US, Savitsky was the captain who *wanted* to launch (not prevent it), and Malenkov was a post-Stalin Soviet politician with no role in this incident."}'::jsonb),
  ('20th-century world history', 'adult', 'mc', 'The Nuremberg Laws of 1935, which stripped Jews of German citizenship and prohibited marriage between Jews and non-Jews, were passed during which annual Nazi Party event?', '{"options":["The Berlin Olympics Congress","The Wannsee Conference","The Nuremberg Rally","The Night of the Long Knives"],"correctIndex":2}'::jsonb, false, 'ready', '{"verdict":"ready","notes":"The Nuremberg Laws were indeed passed on September 15, 1935, at the annual Nazi Party Rally (Reichsparteitag) held in Nuremberg; all distractors are clearly wrong — the Berlin Olympics Congress is not a real event, the Wannsee Conference (1942) planned the Holocaust, and the Night of the Long Knives (1934) was a purge of the SA leadership."}'::jsonb),
